# Suppresses a lock-order-inversion report that ThreadSanitizer raises against
# the fork-prepare handlers of this module and cthreadcomm.
#
# What the report says. Every module here that registers a pthread_atfork()
# prepare handler takes its own locks in that handler and holds them until after
# the fork, so the handlers' locks nest in whichever order the handlers run in.
# The reported cycle pairs clog_slot_table.rwlock, taken in
# _clog_atfork_prepare, against the queue registry mutex taken in
# _cthreadcomm_atfork_prepare, each appearing to be acquired while the other is
# held.
#
# Why it cannot deadlock. The C library runs prepare handlers one sequence at a
# time under its own atfork lock, so two handler sequences never interleave and
# neither can wait on a lock the other holds. Within a process the relative
# order of any two handlers is also fixed, since it is reverse registration
# order and the modules force their dependencies to register ahead of them; the
# two directions of the cycle therefore come from separate processes rather than
# from one lock-order graph that a single process could deadlock on.
# ThreadSanitizer's own graph does not distinguish them, because a forked child
# inherits the parent's graph and keeps adding to it.
#
# The second half of that argument is checked rather than asserted. Each prepare
# handler reports itself to _ccol_atfork_order_record (see ccol_atfork_module_t
# in common.h), which records the pairwise order and aborts if two handlers ever
# run in opposite relative orders, which is the condition that would make this
# cycle real. tests/common's atfork_order tests pin both directions of that
# check. So a genuine inversion stops the run even while this file keeps the
# detector quiet, which is what a suppression over our own code has to earn.
#
# What this file is worth. It silences output and nothing else: the deadlock
# detector's reports do not set the exit status, so the ThreadSanitizer target
# passes with or without it. Keeping it costs the reports; removing it costs a
# handful of lines of noise on every run of this suite.
#
# Anchored on this module's own frame, always symbol-resolved, rather than on
# any pthread-internal frame name, mirroring tests/chttpclient/tsan_openssl.supp's
# own "anchor on our own code" choice. It names _clog_atfork_prepare, which is
# where the write lock is taken: an anchor naming a function that no longer takes
# the lock suppresses nothing, silently, and nothing fails until the reports
# resurface somewhere else.
#
# Know what this hides. The anchor matches every lock-order-inversion report
# carrying that frame, including one between this module's prepare handler and
# another module's that did not arise from an inherited graph. That is the class
# _ccol_atfork_order_record exists to catch independently; anyone changing which
# locks a fork-prepare handler takes, or the order the handlers register in,
# should re-derive the reasoning above rather than trusting a clean
# ThreadSanitizer run here.
deadlock:_clog_atfork_prepare
